sum='Sue and Johnsy, two young artists, shared a small flat on the third floor. In November Johnsy fell seriously ill with pneumonia. The doctor told Sue that Johnsy had made up her mind not to get well – medicines would not help unless she wanted to live. Johnsy lay counting the leaves falling from an old ivy creeper on the brick wall opposite and said she would die when the last leaf fell. Sue told the old painter Behrman, who lived on the ground floor. Behrman, about sixty, had always dreamt of painting a masterpiece. That night a fierce storm blew, and another storm came the next evening, but one leaf still clung to the creeper. Seeing that it did not fall, Johnsy felt ashamed of wanting to die and regained her will to live. The doctor said she would recover, but Behrman was ill with pneumonia; he died after only two days of illness. A ladder, a lantern, brushes and green and yellow paints were found – Behrman had painted the leaf on the wall in the stormy night. That leaf was his masterpiece.',
